(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-2-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology II: Adrenal, Parathyroid & Pancreas — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The three classes of adrenal cortical hormones are mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and:","choices":["Androgenic hormones","Catecholamines","Thyroid hormones","Peptide hormones"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three adrenal cortical hormone classes are mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgenic hormones. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Per the lecture's breakdown of adrenal cortical hormone output, approximately what percentage is aldosterone?","choices":["75%","5%","50%","15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Aldosterone accounts for approximately 15% of adrenal cortical hormone output. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which adrenal androgen precursors make up about 10% of adrenal cortical hormone output?","choices":["Aldosterone and DOC","Cortisol and corticosterone","DHEA and androstenedione","Testosterone and estrogen"],"answer":2,"exp":"DHEA and androstenedione make up approximately 10% of adrenal cortical hormone output. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The 75% category of adrenal cortical hormones (cortisol, corticosterone, androgens, estrogens) is primarily controlled by:","choices":["Aldosterone itself","Renin","Angiotensin II","ACTH"],"answer":3,"exp":"This 75% category, dominated by cortisol, is controlled by ACTH. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Adrenal steroids are cleared via which two general routes described in this lecture?","choices":["Only through direct excretion via eccrine sweat gland secretion","Through the liver into bile/feces and processing involving the kidney","Only through direct breakdown by skeletal muscle metabolism","Only through direct diffusion into the atmosphere via the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adrenal steroid clearance involves the liver (bile/feces route, with glucuronic acid conjugation) and the kidney, alongside plasma handling. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Aldosterone is produced in which zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Zona glomerulosa","Adrenal medulla","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aldosterone is produced in the zona glomerulosa, the outermost zone of the adrenal cortex. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is aldosterone's primary site of action?","choices":["Loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule","Renal collecting duct","Bowman's capsule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone acts primarily at the renal collecting duct. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Structurally, aldosterone is derived from which precursor and is lipid or water soluble?","choices":["Derived from amino acids; water soluble","Derived from cholesterol; lipid soluble","Derived from glucose; lipid soluble","Derived from tyrosine; water soluble"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aldosterone is derived from cholesterol and is lipid soluble. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What percentage of circulating aldosterone is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG)?","choices":["About 100%","About 60%","About 90%","About 10%"],"answer":1,"exp":"About 60% of aldosterone is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG) and other plasma proteins (albumin, prealbumin). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which set of findings is associated with HIGH aldosterone levels?","choices":["Hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability, cell acidosis","Hypercalcemia, diffuse bone pain, and recurrent pathologic fractures","No change in potassium, heart rate, or acid-base status","Hypokalemia, muscle weakness, slow heart rate/arrhythmias, cell alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"High aldosterone is associated with hypokalemia, muscle weakness, slow heart rate/arrhythmias, and cell alkalosis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which set of findings is associated with LOW aldosterone levels?","choices":["Hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability early on, arrhythmias/ventricular fibrillation, cell acidosis","Hypokalemia, progressive generalized muscle weakness, and cell alkalosis with a normal heart rhythm","No measurable physiological consequence on potassium, muscle, or cardiac rhythm","Hypertension and generalized peripheral edema, with no described change in serum potassium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low aldosterone is associated with hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability early on, arrhythmias/ventricular fibrillation, and cell acidosis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of the following increases aldosterone secretion, per this lecture's control summary?","choices":["ACTH acting alone as the single primary rate-limiting regulator","Increased extracellular fluid (ECF) volume acting alone","Decreased renin-angiotensin system (RAS) activity alone","Increased potassium and increased renin-angiotensin system (RAS) activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased potassium and increased RAS activity are the most important regulators that increase aldosterone secretion; ACTH is needed for secretion but does not itself set the rate. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Cortisol is produced in which zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona reticularis","Adrenal medulla","Zona fasciculata"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol is produced in the zona fasciculata, the middle zone of the adrenal cortex. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What percentage of circulating cortisol is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG)?","choices":["About 90%","About 60%","About 10%","About 25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"About 90% of circulating cortisol is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Cortisol's carbohydrate metabolism effects include increased gluconeogenesis and:","choices":["Decreased blood glucose (a hypoglycemic effect)","No change at all in fasting blood glucose","Complete cessation of hepatic glucose production","Increased blood glucose (the best-known metabolic effect)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol increases blood glucose — described as its best-known metabolic effect — via increased gluconeogenesis and moderately decreased glucose utilization. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Cortisol's effect on protein stores is decreased everywhere in the body EXCEPT:","choices":["The liver","Adipose tissue","Skeletal muscle","Skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cortisol decreases protein stores throughout the body except in the liver, where it promotes amino acid uptake, protein synthesis, gluconeogenesis, and plasma protein formation. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Cortisol mobilizes fatty acids for energy partly by:","choices":["Blocking fatty acid oxidation entirely, forcing exclusive reliance on glucose","Decreasing glucose transport into fat cells, shifting fuel use from glucose to fat","Increasing glucose transport into fat cells, favoring fat storage over use","Increasing pancreatic insulin secretion directly in order to mobilize stored fat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cortisol decreases glucose transport into fat cells, which helps shift fuel utilization from glucose to fat, alongside enhancing fatty acid oxidation. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What triggers hypothalamic control of cortisol release, per this lecture's list of stressors?","choices":["Only circadian rhythm, with no acute stress response","Only dietary sodium intake, with no stress-related trigger involved","Only genetic factors, with no environmental triggers","Trauma, infection, heat/cold, surgery, and other stressors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Trauma, infection, heat/cold, sympathetic activation, surgery, and restraint/disease are all listed as stressors triggering hypothalamic control of cortisol release. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Besides acute stress responses, what other pattern controls baseline cortisol secretion?","choices":["Random, unpatterned fluctuation","Lunar cycle","Seasonal variation only, with no daily pattern","Circadian rhythm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol secretion follows a circadian rhythm, in addition to being driven acutely by stress. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Epinephrine, a hormone of the adrenal medulla, acts on which tissues per this lecture?","choices":["The thyroid gland exclusively","Bone and cartilage only","Muscle, liver, and fat","Skin only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epinephrine acts on muscle, liver, and fat. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where is epinephrine produced, per this lecture?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal medulla","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Epinephrine is produced in the adrenal medulla. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Besides insulin and glucagon, which other hormones does the pancreas produce, per this lecture's overview?","choices":["Cortisol, aldosterone, and adrenal androgen hormones","Amylin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide","TSH, ACTH, prolactin, and growth hormone","PTH, calcitonin, and calcitriol (active vitamin D)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pancreas also produces amylin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide, alongside insulin and glucagon. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In the summary of glucose regulation, what is insulin's role?","choices":["Has no relationship to plasma glucose","Increased by low glucose; raises glucose toward normal","Only affects glucose in the kidney","Increased by glucose; returns glucose to normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Insulin is increased by glucose and acts to return glucose to normal (i.e., lower elevated glucose). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In the summary of glucose regulation, what is glucagon's role?","choices":["Produced exclusively by hepatocytes within the liver","Increased by high glucose; lowers glucose further","Increased by low glucose; returns glucose to normal","Functionally identical to insulin's glucose-lowering role"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glucagon is increased by low glucose and acts to return glucose to normal (i.e., raise low glucose). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"During hypoglycemia, which hormone increases via sympathetic nerve stimulation to raise glucose from the liver?","choices":["Aldosterone","Oxytocin","Epinephrine","Insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"During hypoglycemia, sympathetic nerve stimulation increases epinephrine, which increases glucose release from the liver. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Besides epinephrine, hypoglycemia also increases which two other hormones that promote fat utilization and decrease glucose utilization?","choices":["TSH, ACTH, and prolactin","Aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","Growth hormone and cortisol","Insulin and glucagon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hypoglycemia increases growth hormone and cortisol, which promote fat utilization and decrease glucose utilization, complementing epinephrine's response. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the normal fasting plasma glucose range mentioned in this lecture's glucose regulation summary?","choices":["200–220 mg/dL","80–90 mg/dL","40–60 mg/dL","150–170 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture cites 80–90 mg/dL as the normal fasted plasma glucose range that insulin helps maintain. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Besides direct glucose levels, which factor listed also stimulates insulin release via adrenergic mechanisms?","choices":["Only glucose levels, with no other described stimulus","Adrenergic stimulation (norepinephrine, epinephrine)","Cold environmental exposure exclusively, with no other trigger","Only somatostatin, acting alone as an inhibitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adrenergic stimulation (via norepinephrine and epinephrine) is listed among the factors affecting insulin secretion, alongside glucose, somatostatin, GI hormones (secretin, GLP-1), free fatty acids, and ketoacids. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which GI hormone, alongside secretin, is listed as affecting insulin secretion?","choices":["Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)","Gastrin, a stomach-derived hormone","Motilin, a GI motility hormone","Cholecystokinin (CCK)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), alongside secretin, is listed among the GI hormones affecting insulin secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"The major actions of parathyroid hormone (PTH) include increasing plasma calcium and increasing renal production of which active compound?","choices":["Aldosterone, a mineralocorticoid hormone","Angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor peptide","Calcitonin, PTH's functional antagonist","1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active vitamin D)"],"answer":3,"exp":"PTH increases renal production of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active vitamin D/calcitriol), alongside directly increasing plasma calcium and promoting calcium/phosphate release from bone. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 91"}]};
